ff this flavor bacterium  but there are actually two more zones of  inhibition that you can  pick up and you see them now circled in  the yellow circle right there  and so here you have on this one plate  out of these 48 strains we got  the three different isolates that  inhibit the growth  able to inhibit the growth of flavor  bacterium columnar  and so of the 562 isolates that we  screened  we found a 35 isolates that inhibited at  least  one pathogen so that's basically six  percent of the pathogens  uh inhibit six percent of the isolates  that we screened inhibited at least  one of these pathogens uh 18 out of the  35  actually inhibited two or more pathogens  and five of them  three pathogens and so we have now a  collection of strains that we would like  to analyze further  and the first thing is we want to  identify what these bacteria are  and for that we returned to the  shoreline biome  b1 through b9 kit we used it for the dna  extractions  they're using the dna print in 96 well  format  so when we grew them up we could go  straight from the 96 well plate that we  grew them up in  to the dna prep plate and after  isolating the dna we would then put it  on the bar-coded pcr plate  and then we had six different plates but  they all have the same  96 barcodes and so what we did is  we used six different smart belts that  had different barcodes  and that was done at the university of  maryland for us at their sequencing  center  and so they prepared the six different  smart bells  that gave us then six different barcodes  one unique barcode for each  pcr plate they were all pooled and then  they were sequenced on a single  sequel to run  we got the data back and we got  millions of reads back and then  if you now look how many reads we got  for each  of the smart bells and for each of the  samples we got over 4  000 reads for each one of our 96  samples and so this gives us a really  deep insight  into the 16s sequences that we get from  there and so we could have actually  multiplex uh you know gone  to 12 plates for example so  we have a very good depth it also allows  us to detect contamination  or if we have two isolates in one plate  we identified them by using the  sv analyzer and data ii and after that  the asvs were incorporated into chime 2  and there we used the green gene  reference data set initially  to identify them and then more recently  we used the silver and  database and that actually gave us a  more detailed higher resolution  identification  and so then for each genus we took all  of the  sequin asbs that we got back and all of  the 16s  rna sequences of the type strains and  constructed phylogenetic trees  that we could take a closer look at the  phylogenetic relationships and the  identification  here's the overall taxonomic breakdown  so we cultured bacteria belonging to 22  different families and 48 different  genera  the most common genera included  pseudomonas pheromonous  flavor bacterium and a cynicobacter  and the most the ice the isolates that  inhibited the bacteria most of them  actually belong to pseudomonas  and so if you think back now to the  earlier slide where i showed you the  changes in the microbial community of  the raceway as the fish  grew the young the biofilms in the  raceways containing the youngest fish  actually had the most  number of flavor of pseudomonas and then  later on they were less abundant  so these pseudomonads seem to produce  antibiotics  but they're not that abundant in the  older biofilms  here you can now see i'm going to show  you a couple of phylogenetic trees  and i begin with the zoom in to kind of  explain it to you a little bit  and so in the center you see the  phylogenetic tree  in blue are the type strains and in red  are the asvs that you  found and they actually represent  most likely novel species because  they're quite different  from the known type strains and then  when you go to the  outer circles the red color and the blue  color there again  indicate an asb versus a type strain and  then the squares  show you which species of pathogens each  isolate  inhibited so here  you have a bacterium an isolate that is  closely related  uh to a  pseudomonas and destiny and that one  actually inhibited you yosemia rockery  and flavor bacterium cyclophyllum okay  and so this way we can kind of  depict how many different bacteria we  cultured and how diverse the microbial  community is that they came from  and i'm going to zoom out now to show  you how many different  types of pseudomonas we cultured and how  far they're spread out over the  phylogenetic tree  so here you see the blue lines uh at the  outset outermost edge  and i'll show you how many isolates we  got for each  asv and here i'm just kind of  highlighting  different species that these isolates  were closely related to so we got a  large  diversity of pseudomonas that we could  culture and many of them  were actually able to inhibit the growth  of at least one of these four pathogens  we also did something similar for flavor  bacteria  and you can see right here is the  phylogenetic tree of the flavor  bacteria and the blue lines again  will show you how many isolates we got  the  asbs that are related to the species  that i'm showing you there for example  the flavor bacterium aquali and that was  also able to inhibit the growth of some  bacteria  and so using this bio full length 16s  rna  based sequencing approach we were able  to identify  a very large number of bacteria in a  very quick way  and and get a real good insight into the  composition  of the microbial communities  and so the biofilm in aquaculture  settings undergo  microbial succession this includes  changes in the species composition  and also an increase in diversity and  this is what one would expect from  biofilms in other environments as well  but this is a real com  complex microbial community  and we also were able to identify  bacteria that were able to inhibit  the growth of other bacteria  and pseudomonas for example is one of  them  and especially pseudomonas was then most  abundant in the biofilms of the race  weight of the youngest fish and so if we  can find  ways perhaps to seed the environment  with pseudomonas or  keep them more abundant we could prevent  the invasion of these biofilms by more  pathogenic bacteria  the full-length 16s rna sequencing and  b4 sequencing  provided yielded different community  structures which might be  probably due to differences in the  primary biases  or also the cell lysis approach  the full length 16s rna sequencing using  the pac bio was a really efficient way  to identify hundreds of isolates in one  big experiment  so instead of doing a whole bunch of  traditional  sanger 16s sequencing having to  align all of these sequences to get a  consensus sequence  consequence and then identify them we  were able to do one big experiment get  full length sequences that are highly  accurate  and identify hundreds of bacteria in one  big  experiment i would like to  acknowledge especially todd testament  stephanie valencia and  and also haley donahue which is not on  this slide yet  was not on this slide yet for really  working on this project and haley did a  really big effort  in culturing these bacteria and  many members of my lab assisted in that  also in idaho we have the help from  stephen rightly and stacy king  this is supported by the usda we worked  with greg wiens and tim welch on this  and shoreline biome was very helpful in  helping us in our analysis  pipeline and mark driscoll was important  in that and eric jackson  was instrumental in doing some of the  data analysis  thank you much and are happy to take any  questions by email


